In C++ programming, the `if-else if` ladder allows us to check multiple conditions in sequence. This control structure is useful when we have more than two possible outcomes, and we want to execute different code blocks based on which condition is true.

**Syntax**
```cpp
if (condition1) {
    // Code if condition1 is true
}
else if (condition2) {
    // Code if condition2 is true
}
else if (condition3) {
    // Code if condition3 is true
}
else {
    // Code if none of the above conditions are true
}
```
---

**Example**
```cpp
#include 
using namespace std;

int main() {
    int deliveryDays = 2;

    if (deliveryDays <= 1) {
        cout << "Same-day delivery!" << endl;
    }
    else if (deliveryDays <= 3) {
        cout << "Priority delivery!" << endl;
    }
    else if (deliveryDays <= 7) {
        cout << "Standard delivery." << endl;
    }
    else {
        cout << "Delayed delivery." << endl;
    }

    return 0;
}
```
**Expected Output**
